title: View/Edit Geometry
keyword: EDIT
manual: structure/#c2-edit

Inspect and edit the structure of a system: its species, atoms, unit cell, molecules,
bonds and symmetry. Every change is applied to the system immediately. "Restore" returns
the system to the geometry it had when it was first opened, and "Keep bonding" preserves
the bond connectivity when the geometry changes.

# Tabs
- Species: one row per species. Click the atomic number to move the atoms to another
  species or change the element; "New" adds a species.
- Atoms: the atoms and their coordinates. "Types" groups the atoms by category and "Keep
  symmetry" makes coordinate changes respect the space group. "Expression" evaluates an
  expression at the atomic positions. "All", "None" and "Toggle" change the selection;
  "Add", "Duplicate", "Remove", "Merge" and "Reorder" act on the selected atoms.
- Cell (crystals only): the lattice parameters (a/b/c, α/β/γ, volume) are editable, with
  "Keep symmetry" to keep the space group. "Standard cells" goes to the conventional,
  primitive, Niggli or Delaunay cell; "Refine positions" (under More) also moves the atoms
  to their ideal symmetry positions. "Supercell" prepares a new cell in one of four modes
  and shows it in the view, with its size, number of atoms and cell parameters; "Apply"
  transforms the crystal. "Simple" is a na/nb/nc repetition. "Matrix" takes the lattice
  vectors of the new cell, typed or picked in the view, and an origin; "Inverse" (under
  More) uses the inverse of the matrix. "Search" lists, for each size up to "Max. size",
  the best supercell by the chosen criterion: "Niceness" (the largest inscribed sphere,
  the closest to a cube) or "Reach" (the longest shortest lattice vector). "Fewest
  displacements" restricts the choice to the supercells that need the fewest displaced
  structures in a phonon calculation. Click a row to select it; "Sublattices of size n"
  then lists every distinct supercell of that size, to choose another one. "Orient" finds
  a cell with two axes in a family of lattice planes (hkl) and the third perpendicular to
  them (if the lattice has such a vector), or with one axis along a lattice direction
  [uvw]; the indices refer to the current cell. With "Primitive cell", the third axis of
  the plane cell goes to the next plane instead, as close to the normal as possible, and
  the cell is primitive. "Vacuum" sets the empty space along an axis: the cell is cut by
  a plane parallel to the other two axes and grows or shrinks there, without changing the
  distances between the atoms. The cut is in the middle of the largest gap along the axis
  or, with "Position", at the given fractional coordinate (the atoms below it go to the
  top of the slab) or, with "Atom", at an atom clicked in the view, which is left at the
  bottom of the slab; the plane of the cut is shown in the view. The slab is left in the
  center or at the bottom of the new cell; under More, "Orthogonal axis" makes the axis
  perpendicular to the other two and "Keep molecules whole" avoids cutting molecules
  (the cut then divides their centers, shown as spheres in the view; it is on at the
  start in a molecular crystal).
  The view shows the outline of the new cell while the vacuum is dragged or typed, and
  the cell changes when the drag ends or the value is entered; the slab position and the
  options apply at once. A cut at a position or at an atom is prepared with all its
  settings and made with "Apply". "Reset" returns to the vacuum the cell had when the
  system or the axis was chosen here, or when the lattice was last changed by another
  tool or by undo.
  "Origin" moves the origin of the cell, with the same lattice vectors, to an atom picked
  in the view, to the center of mass, to the closest inversion center, or to the point
  that centers the atoms along the axes that have vacuum. While this tab is open, the view
  shows the whole cell along the axes that have vacuum, which the unit cell object cuts
  around the slab otherwise.
- Molecules: the molecular fragments with their number of atoms, point group and center of
  mass. Selected molecules can be removed or reordered.
- Bonds: "Bond operations" create, remove or change the order of bonds by clicking in the
  view. "System bonds" lists the bonds of every atom (single, double, triple, aromatic or
  dashed) with a context menu per bond. "Bond calculation" sets the covalent radii, the
  bond factor, the bond delta and the allowed pairs; "Apply" recomputes the connectivity.
- Symmetry: for molecules, the point group and its operations, with "Refine" to move the
  atoms onto their exact symmetry positions. For crystals, the space group, the detection
  tolerance with "Recalculate", "Clear", "Refine" and "Whole Molecules", the operations and
  centering vectors (select them to create a symmetry-elements object or delete them to
  build a subgroup), and "Space group analysis", which scans the space group as a function
  of the tolerance.
